What v2 still fails to establish
1. The trust moved from the model to the harness, and nothing binds evidence to real reviewers. Every load-bearing artifact — round-1 findings, the cross-feed event log, hashes, timestamps, the reconciliation itself — is produced or recorded by the harness. A run in which the harness fabricates both lineages' findings, logs its own cross-feed events, hashes its own artifacts, and reconciles with itself is green on every evidence line in this card. v1's fraud was reviewers who couldn't measure; v2's residual fraud is a review that never happened. Given that the card's reason to exist is Eric calling the review fraudulent, this is in scope. Fix: bind artifacts to sessions — per-lineage transcripts with session IDs attributable to 8649/8650, tool-call logs, and the cross-feed log referencing those session artifacts.

2. FAIL-CLOSED is a claim with no test. The done-when enumerates seven failure modes — hook failure, unknown tool, unparseable command, reconciliation crash, empty lineage, irreconcilable disagreement, dispatch attempt. The EVIDENCE section tests at most one or two. No kill-the-hook-mid-session test, no crash-the-reconciliation test, no empty-lineage run, no unknown-tool probe (the negative suite tests known-dangerous commands, which is a blacklist-shaped test of a default-deny property). This is v1's signature disease — done-whens without evidence — recurring inside the section written to cure it. And fail-closed is precisely the property most likely to be wrong in practice, because it only manifests when something breaks, i.e., when nobody's checking.

3. "Unchanged filesystem hash" is a mechanism, not a spec. Hash of what, over which watch-set? A whole-filesystem hash is self-contradictory — the hook log must change to record the denial, so the test's own success token breaks its hash check. A single canary is vacuous. Specify: a manifest hash over a declared watch-set (allowed read paths + canary write targets), excluding log paths, captured before and after each probe.

4. The subagent OR-fork contradicts the evidence. DONE WHEN permits "no subagent spawn or subagents inherit the constrained profile." The negative suite requires spawn to be denied with a hook-log entry. An implementer who picks the inheritance branch fails the evidence for a compliant implementation — or quietly skips the probe. Pick one branch; if inheritance, the probe becomes "spawn a subagent, verify the child's schema dump matches the constrained profile" — a different test that currently doesn't exist.

5. "Run read-only validation" contradicts "no interpreters." The measurement enumeration includes running validation; the terminal policy bans interpreters and the BUILD says "mediated" tools. Either validation is a mediated harness tool (say so, name it) or this bullet quietly reintroduces a code-execution surface — and any code execution is a write surface (test suites write caches, temp files, coverage data).

6. Evidence is procedure, not artifact. Nothing requires the probes to be a committed, re-runnable script that emits a hashed report. v1 at least said "must actually run" on one line; v2 dropped even that. Name the runner (e.g., tools/readonly_probes.sh), the output report, its location, and make DONE contingent on the report existing. Evidence that can't be re-run is testimony — same rule as v1.

7. Smaller residuals: no probe that the staging path is denied to the other reviewer before cross-feed (the independence guarantee's enforcement moment); no canary-secret test (plant a secret in an allowed path, verify it never appears in reviewer output — this tests both redaction and injection-exfiltration in one probe); grader identity for the spine-fact question (harness ground truth, not a model, or the compliance-vs-enforcement problem recurs one level up); the negative suite needs a disposable fixture (a git checkout probe against the real repo is destructive if the wall fails); and CLARITY now argues with v1 ("as originally written") — an implementer who never saw v1 can't parse that, which dents the self-containment the card is supposed to have. State requirements; don't rebut ghosts.

